{"name":"Jack Rabbit","hex":"#c0b2b1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jack-rabbit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of jackrabbit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jack-rabbit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Antelope jackrabbit","blocks":[{"text":"The antelope jackrabbit, also known as Allen's hare, is a species of North American hare in the family Leporidae found in southern Arizona and northwestern Mexico. Considered the \"handsomest\" of the hares, it occupies dry desert areas and was one of the last of the North American mammals to be formally described."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Lepus_alleni_83745875_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Lepus_alleni_83745875_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antelope_jackrabbit","wikidata":"Q577630"},{"title":"Black-tailed jackrabbit","blocks":[{"text":"The black-tailed jackrabbit, also known as the American desert hare, is a common hare of the western United States and Mexico, where it is found at elevations from sea level up to 10,000 ft (3,000 m). Reaching a length around 2 ft (61 cm), and a weight from 3 to 6 lb, the black-tailed jackrabbit is one of the largest North American hares. Black-tailed jackrabbits occupy mixed shrub-grassland terrains. Their breeding depends on the location; it typically peaks in spring, but may continue all year round in warm climates. Young are born fully furred with eyes open; they are well camouflaged and are mobile within minutes of birth, thus females do not protect or even stay with the young except during nursing. The average litter size is around four, but may be as low as two and as high as seven in warm regions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Black-tailed_Jack_Rabbit_%28Lepus_californicus%29.jpg/330px-Black-tailed_Jack_Rabbit_%28Lepus_californicus%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black-tailed_jackrabbit","wikidata":"Q640645"},{"title":"Jack Rabbit (Seabreeze)","blocks":[{"text":"Jack Rabbit is a wooden roller coaster at Seabreeze Amusement Park in Irondequoit, New York. Jack Rabbit is a terrain coaster that features seven dips, a turnaround, and a tunnel. It opened on May 31, 1920. Jack Rabbit is the oldest operating coaster in the United States, as the oldest standing, Leap-The-Dips, has not operated since 2023. Jack Rabbit is also the third oldest operating roller coaster in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Jack-Rabbit-Seabreeze-1.jpg/330px-Jack-Rabbit-Seabreeze-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Rabbit_(Seabreeze)","wikidata":"Q1677168"},{"title":"Jack Rabbit (Kennywood)","blocks":[{"text":"Jack Rabbit is a wooden roller coaster located at Kennywood Park in West Mifflin, Pennsylvania. Designed and built by John A. Miller and Harry C. Baker, Jack Rabbit opened in 1920, making it the fifth oldest roller coaster in the world. It is also the second oldest operating roller coaster in the United States. The ride's three trains were manufactured by Edward Vettel Sr. in 1951 and contain three cars of six seats each. A popular early feature of the ride was a tunnel which covered the turnaround section after the first drop, but this was removed in 1947. In 1991, the tunnel was restored at a slightly shorter length."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Kennywood_Jack_Rabbit_DSCN2775.JPG/330px-Kennywood_Jack_Rabbit_DSCN2775.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Rabbit_(Kennywood)","wikidata":"Q1677171"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Antelope_Jackrabbit_area.png/330px-Antelope_Jackrabbit_area.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Antelope_Jackrabbit_area.png/330px-Antelope_Jackrabbit_area.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntelope_Jackrabbit_area.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Lepus_alleni_palitans_-_Juan_Cruzado_Cort%C3%A9s_-_2492856.jpeg/500px-Lepus_alleni_palitans_-_Juan_Cruzado_Cort%C3%A9s_-_2492856.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Lepus_alleni_palitans_-_Juan_Cruzado_Cort%C3%A9s_-_2492856.jpeg/1280px-Lepus_alleni_palitans_-_Juan_Cruzado_Cort%C3%A9s_-_2492856.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Lepus_alleni_palitans_-_Juan_Cruzado_Cort%C3%A9s_-_2492856.jpeg/500px-Lepus_alleni_palitans_-_Juan_Cruzado_Cort%C3%A9s_-_2492856.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An antelope jackrabbit of subspecies L. a. palitans","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALepus_alleni_palitans_-_Juan_Cruzado_Cort%C3%A9s_-_2492856.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Lepus_alleni_Mazatlan_500458.jpg/500px-Lepus_alleni_Mazatlan_500458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Lepus_alleni_Mazatlan_500458.jpg/500px-Lepus_alleni_Mazatlan_500458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An antelope jackrabbit and a purplish-backed jay seen together","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALepus_alleni_Mazatlan_500458.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/